<p>I'm writing these monthly briefings looking back over 2026 so far, picking out what actually changes decisions for teams building AI, data and platform systems. April was not a month of flashy launches for my work. It was a month where several long-running pieces of plumbing finally became safe defaults, and that is usually what matters more.</p>
<p>All of the platform items below come from the projects' own release posts. Where I mention model releases or regulation, I've cited the coverage I relied on.</p>

<section class="block">
<h2>The month in one paragraph</h2>
<p>Kubernetes v1.36 shipped on 22 April with 70 enhancements, 18 of them graduating to stable<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>, and the headline graduations were security ones: user namespaces and fine-grained kubelet authorisation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup><sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. Gateway API v1.5 moved six features to its stable channel<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. Google released Gemma 4 under Apache 2.0<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>, Anthropic released Claude Opus 4.7<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>, MCP expanded its maintainer team<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>, OpenTelemetry formally deprecated OpenTracing compatibility<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>, and CMS proposed FHIR standards for prior authorisation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-10">10</a></sup>.</p>
<table class="tbl">
<thead><tr><th>Item</th><th>What changed</th><th>Why it matters</th></tr></thead>
<tbody>
<tr><td>User namespaces (K8s 1.36)</td><td>GA; set <code>hostUsers: false</code></td><td>Root in a container is no longer root on the node</td></tr>
<tr><td>Kubelet authorisation (K8s 1.36)</td><td>Fine-grained permissions GA</td><td>Monitoring agents no longer need <code>nodes/proxy</code></td></tr>
<tr><td>Gateway API v1.5</td><td>ListenerSet, TLSRoute, CORS and more to stable</td><td>Multi-team ingress without sharing one object</td></tr>
<tr><td>Gemma 4</td><td>Apache 2.0 licence</td><td>Self-hosted models pass legal review faster</td></tr>
<tr><td>CMS proposed rule</td><td>FHIR for prior authorisation</td><td>FHIR moves from patient access into payer workflows</td></tr>
</tbody>
</table>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Kubernetes 1.36: the security defaults grew up</h2>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/briefings/k8s-v1-36.webp" alt="Kubernetes v1.36 Haru release logo: a hex badge over Mount Fuji with two cats wearing the Kubernetes helm" width="1327" height="1328" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The v1.36 release logo, inspired by Hokusai's Thirty-six Views of Mount Fuji. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://kubernetes.io/blog/2026/04/22/kubernetes-v1-36-release/" target="_blank" rel="noopener">Kubernetes blog, Kubernetes v1.36: ハル (Haru)</a> · CC BY 4.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>Two graduations stood out to me. The first is <strong>user namespaces</strong>. The post puts the problem plainly: a process running as root inside a container is seen by the kernel as root on the host, so a container escape lands an attacker as root<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-2">2</a></sup>. With <code>hostUsers: false</code> the container's root is mapped to an unprivileged range on the host, and capabilities such as <code>CAP_NET_ADMIN</code> become namespaced. The enabling work was in the kernel: ID-mapped mounts remove the expensive recursive <code>chown</code> that used to make this impractical for large volumes.</p>
<p>The second is <strong>fine-grained kubelet API authorisation</strong>. Before it, almost every kubelet API path mapped to <code>nodes/proxy</code>, the same permission that allows running commands in any container on the node<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-3">3</a></sup>. The post also notes research from early 2026 showing that even <code>nodes/proxy GET</code> could be abused through WebSocket upgrades. In plain terms, many clusters have handed node-level superuser rights to their monitoring agents. That is now avoidable.</p>
<p>The release also permanently disabled the <code>gitRepo</code> volume driver and deprecated Service <code>externalIPs</code>, with removal planned for v1.43<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>. Both are long-known security liabilities.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Gateway API v1.5: ingress for more than one team</h2>
<p>Gateway API v1.5 promoted six features to its standard channel, including ListenerSet, TLSRoute, the HTTPRoute CORS filter and client certificate validation<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-4">4</a></sup>. ListenerSet is the one I care about. Previously every listener lived on the Gateway object itself, so platform and application teams had to coordinate changes to the same resource. ListenerSet lets application teams attach their own listeners from their own namespaces.</p>
<p>The project also moved to a release-train model, where features that are ready (including their documentation) ship at feature freeze. With Ingress NGINX retired in March and no longer receiving security fixes<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-1">1</a></sup>, the migration path to Gateway API is now both available and urgent.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Models: an open licence that legal teams can sign</h2>
<p>Google released Gemma 4, describing it as the first Gemma family under the OSI-approved Apache 2.0 licence<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-5">5</a></sup>. For the work I do with regulated clients, the licence matters more than the benchmark tables. Custom model licences slow every procurement review. An Apache 2.0 model can go through the same process as any other open-source dependency, which makes private, self-hosted deployments far easier to approve. It is exactly the class of model I have been testing on my own hardware, which I wrote about in my <a href="/insights/private-llm-on-prem/">notes on private LLMs</a>.</p>
<p>At the hosted end, Anthropic released Claude Opus 4.7 on 16 April, positioned for complex reasoning, agentic coding and long-running tasks, with a new effort level and task budgets for controlling token use in agent loops<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-6">6</a></sup>. Budget controls like that are the kind of feature that moves agents from demo to production, because cost per task becomes something you can cap rather than discover.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Agents and observability</h2>
<p>MCP expanded its governance team, adding a new Lead Maintainer whose work centres on authorisation and security, and a new Core Maintainer from agent developer tooling<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-7">7</a></sup>. Governance news rarely gets attention, but for a protocol that enterprises are wiring into production systems, who maintains the security model matters.</p>
<p>On the observability side, OpenTelemetry deprecated the OpenTracing compatibility requirements in its specification<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-8">8</a></sup>. Existing shims keep working for now, but new work should target native OpenTelemetry APIs and OTLP. Separately, OpenTelemetry eBPF Instrumentation v0.7.0 added HTTP header enrichment, so spans can carry request context such as a tenant ID through a configuration change, without redeploying applications<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-9">9</a></sup>. For multi-tenant platforms, "which customer is affected?" is usually the first question in an incident.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>Healthcare: FHIR reaches prior authorisation</h2>
<p>A CMS fact sheet dated 10 April describes a proposed rule in which HHS would adopt HL7 FHIR standards for prior authorisation transactions under HIPAA's administrative simplification provisions, and require payers to report their interoperability API endpoints<sup class="cite"><a href="#ref-10">10</a></sup>. It is a proposal, not a final rule, but the direction is consistent: FHIR is becoming the transaction layer across US healthcare, not only the patient-access layer.</p>
<figure class="fig"><div class="im"><img src="/assets/img/briefings/ohdsi-cdm.webp" alt="Diagram of the OMOP Common Data Model showing clinical data, vocabulary, health system and derived element tables" width="1400" height="1103" loading="lazy"></div><figcaption>The OMOP Common Data Model, the analytical counterpart to FHIR's exchange layer. <span class="credit">Source: <a href="https://ohdsi.github.io/TheBookOfOhdsi/CommonDataModel.html" target="_blank" rel="noopener">OHDSI, The Book of OHDSI: The Common Data Model</a> · CC0 1.0</span></figcaption></figure>
<p>My view, from working with OMOP and the OHDSI tools: FHIR for exchange and OMOP for analysis remain complementary. More FHIR transactions mean more structured data arriving, and you still need a standardised analytical model to do research and AI on it. I set out that architecture in <a href="/insights/fhir-to-omop-architecture/">FHIR to OMOP</a>.</p>
</section>

<section class="block">
<h2>What I'd do with this</h2>
<ol>
<li><strong>Audit who holds <code>nodes/proxy</code>.</strong> Once on v1.36, move monitoring and logging agents to the fine-grained permissions and remove the broad grant.</li>
<li><strong>Pilot <code>hostUsers: false</code></strong> on stateless workloads first, then on anything that handles untrusted input.</li>
<li><strong>Plan the Ingress NGINX exit</strong> to Gateway API, using ListenerSet to give teams their own listeners.</li>
<li><strong>Add an Apache-licensed model to your private AI shortlist</strong>, and test it against your own evaluation set rather than public leaderboards.</li>
<li><strong>If you handle US payer data</strong>, start mapping prior authorisation flows to FHIR now rather than waiting for a final rule.</li>
</ol>
</section>

